# MXFS filesystem test-suite manifest — THE INDEX (FS-on-mount tests ONLY).
#
# Every test here operates on the MOUNTED filesystem via a mount point and is
# meaningful across N nodes. Device/module/lifecycle/substrate/benchmark
# operations (mkfs, fsck, resize, dkms, raw-block coherency, transport verify,
# vs-XFS benchmark, mount/unmount timing) are NOT here — see tests/tooling/.
#
# Order of lines = run order. Columns (whitespace-separated):
#   PHASE  TEST  COORD  MINNODES  BUDGET_S  [SCALE]
#     COORD    : none | barrier | ordered | fault  (harness coordination needed)
#     MINNODES : minimum nodes the test needs. The runner SKIPS (status SKIPPED)
#                any test whose MINNODES > the run's node count.
#     BUDGET_S : per-CLAUDE.md RULE 0 time budget in seconds — the harness uses
#                this as the test's hard timeout AND records elapsed_s/budget_s
#                into criteria.json; elapsed_s > budget_s flips a PASS to FAIL
#                even with zero correctness errors. Values sourced from
#                tests/criteria/TIMEOUT_BUDGETS.md where a healthy wall was
#                already recorded there; the rest are reasoned initial
#                estimates pending a real measured tightening pass (see that
#                file's "(record)" convention) — do not treat them as final.
#     SCALE    : flat (default, omit) | linear. flat = BUDGET_S applies as-is
#                regardless of node count. linear = BUDGET_S is a PER-NODE
#                seconds coefficient; actual budget = BUDGET_S * N. Use linear
#                ONLY where the test body actually does O(N) work per node
#                (every node cross-verifies every OTHER node's data with a
#                non-trivial per-check cost) — checked against each test's
#                source, not assumed. dir_reuse_coherency is the proven
#                extreme case of this and keeps its own bespoke per-transport
#                formula in run.sh (100*N tcp / 140*N caw) rather than this
#                column, since its coefficient differs by DLM transport.
#
# '#' comments and blank lines ignored.

# --- P(-1) cluster prep, tracked like any other test (2026-07-14) ---
# prep_cluster is NOT dispatched through tests/$cat/$name.sh like a real test
# -- run.sh intercepts it specially, before the main test loop, and calls
# prep_cluster()/prep_cluster_xfs() directly. This manifest line exists only
# so criteria.json has a slot for record() to write into: prep's own
# elapsed_s/budget_s, tracked with the same RULE 0 discipline as every real
# test instead of being an invisible, unbudgeted side effect (see
# ccmemory/session notes 2026-07-14 -- a hung 100s convergence gate was
# previously invisible to showstat entirely). Placeholder budget pending
# real calibration across N -- teardown+mkfs+mount+converge scales with N
# (the membership convergence gate alone is 90+5*N).
P0  prep_cluster          none      1   300
# --- P0 per-node readiness gate ---
# precond_readiness..fault_enospc: budgets below are REAL RULE 0 numbers, not
# estimates -- measured against native XFS on this exact LUN via
# `./run.sh 1 xfs` (2026-07-14): precond_readiness=1s, posix_single=1s,
# fsx=10s, fio_verify=4s, integrity_filetypes=2s, fio_perf=10s,
# fault_enospc=1s. Budget = 2x that measured native wall, floored at 10s to
# absorb ssh-dispatch jitter (see ./showstat.sh 1 xfs to reproduce/refresh).
# 2026-07-19 refresh (ccloop 72513a13 sess10): host under sustained load
# (loadavg ~6-22) runs the SAME native yardstick at fsx=44s fio_verify=21s
# (native fio seqW 525 vs 1116 MiB/s on 07-14) -- native XFS itself failed
# the old 25s/10s budgets.  Re-derived from today's measured native x2:
# fsx 25->88, fio_verify 10->42.  mxfs walls (32s/20s) beat today's native.
P0  precond_readiness     none      1   10

# --- P1 single-node correctness ---
P1  posix_single          none      1   15
P1  fsx                   none      1   88
P1  fio_verify            none      1   42
P1  integrity_filetypes   none      1   10

# --- P1b single-node throughput (4 canonical fio workloads on the mount) ---
# fio_perf: coord=barrier, unbounded max_nodes (2026-07-14) -- at N>1 every
# node runs the same 4 workloads into its OWN private file concurrently,
# publishes its numbers, and rank 1 sums them into an AGGREGATE bandwidth/iops
# figure recorded to bench.json (per-node measured detail isn't surfaced by
# showstat for coord=barrier tests -- same as scaling_curve/dlm_scaling --
# but the aggregate lands in bench.json for fio_perf_vs_xfs to read).
# budget_scale=linear (2026-07-18, ccloop 72513a13): every node pushes the
# same fixed per-node byte load through ONE shared fixed-bandwidth target,
# so the saturation wall grows ~linearly with N.  Measured: 1 node 14s,
# 32-node dm-multipath 328s, 32-node single-path direct iSCSI >600s (26/32
# done at the old flat ceiling; zero errors — pure pacing).  30*N keeps
# 2/4/8/16 walls (15/19/23/58s) and both 32-node rigs inside budget.
# Per-node throughput QUALITY is gated separately by fio_perf_vs_xfs (>=70%
# of the native-XFS single-node baseline).
P1  fio_perf              barrier   1   120
# fio_perf_vs_xfs: separate pass/fail line -- AGGREGATE mxfs throughput
# (single value at N=1) vs the single-node native-XFS baseline
# (.xfs_fio_baseline.json). Reads fio_perf's own bench.json entry for the
# CURRENT node count from this same run, no duplicate fio workload. Must run
# AFTER fio_perf. coord=none: this test itself needs no coordination, it only
# reads what fio_perf already aggregated and published.
P1  fio_perf_vs_xfs       none      1   10

# --- P2 coherency / consistency (multi-node) ---
# REAL RULE 0 numbers below, measured via RULE0_CALIBRATE=1 ./run.sh 2 caw
# (2026-07-14): cache_coherency=4s, strong_consistency=1s, posix_multi=3s,
# mmap_coherency=1s, zero_silent_loss=1s -- all at N=2. Budgets are ~7-10x the
# measured wall (generous first-calibration margin; only one N sampled so
# far). cache_coherency/zero_silent_loss keep linear scaling (proven O(N)
# cross-verify loops in source) with the coefficient now derived from this
# real N=2 point instead of the old TIMEOUT_BUDGETS.md-derived guess; tighten
# further once 4/8/16/32-node data comes in and confirms/refutes the slope.
# min_nodes=1 (2026-07-14) for cache_coherency/strong_consistency/posix_multi/
# mmap_coherency/zero_silent_loss: all checked source-level safe at T=1 (no
# hard multi-node assumption; strong_consistency already had an explicit
# T==1 "role=both" branch). Gives a no-coordination-cost floor, same
# reasoning as dir_reuse_coherency above.
P2  cache_coherency       barrier   1   60
P2  strong_consistency    ordered   1   30
# posix_multi/mmap_coherency also loop for-n-in-T, but each check is a cheap
# stat/cat (posix_multi) or single python mmap-verify (mmap_coherency) -- flat
# is a reasonable approximation until N is large enough to matter; revisit if
# elapsed_s trends up with N.
P2  posix_multi           barrier   1   30
P2  mmap_coherency         ordered   1   30
P2  zero_silent_loss      barrier   1   60

# --- P3 DLM behavior (multi-node) ---
# Measured (2/caw): dlm_fairness=2s, dlm_membership=3s.
# dlm_fairness: min_nodes=1, source-checked safe (no hard multi-node
# assumption). dlm_membership stays min_nodes=2 -- it iptables-blocks the DLM
# TCP port to simulate losing a PEER; with no peer there's nothing to lose,
# the test would be exercising nothing.
P3  dlm_fairness          barrier   1   30
P3  dlm_membership        fault     2   30

# --- P4 scale (multi-node) ---
# scaling_curve/dlm_scaling: each node does a FIXED-size private workload
# (own subdir/file, own internal WINDOW constant baked into the script) in
# parallel -- genuinely N-independent, not a function of node count. Measured
# (2/caw): scaling_curve=2s, dlm_scaling=12s -- both have their own internal
# WINDOW=60s ceiling in the script itself, so the budget floors at ~90s
# (window + margin) rather than a small multiple of the fast measured wall.
# min_nodes=1 (2026-07-14): both scripts' "aggregate > max single node"
# check is mathematically always-false at T=1 (aggregate == the one node's
# own rate, can't exceed itself) -- fixed in-script to skip/relax that check
# when T==1, mirroring strong_consistency's existing T==1 special case.
P4  scaling_curve         barrier   1   90
P4  dlm_scaling           barrier   1   90

# --- P5 performance (on-mount workload, multi-node) ---
# rsync_paired: each node rsyncs into its OWN subdir, no cross-node verify
# loop -- N-independent. Measured (2/caw): 4s; own internal RSYNC_WINDOW=90s.
# min_nodes=1 (2026-07-14): source-checked safe.
P5  rsync_paired          barrier   1   60

# --- P6 fault injection (node-level faults; do NOT touch the block device) ---
P6  fault_enospc          none      1   10
# crash_consistency has the SAME for-n-in-T cross-verify shape as
# cache_coherency (md5sum of real payloads per file). TIMEOUT_BUDGETS.md
# historically documented ~106-108s at N=2/4 (mount+writes+fixed sleep-60+
# reboot). Freshly measured via RULE0_CALIBRATE=1 (2026-07-14): only 5s at
# N=2 -- a large discrepancy from that old number, unexplained (script may
# have changed since, or the old number reflected a different code path).
# NOT aggressively tightened pending that discrepancy being understood --
# kept at a moderate 90s (down from 150s, nowhere near as tight as the 5s
# measurement alone would justify). min_nodes=1 (2026-07-14): source-checked
# safe -- doesn't actually reboot anything (no virsh/reboot in the script),
# just write+cold-reload+cross-check, same shape as cache_coherency.
P6  crash_consistency     fault     1   90
# min_nodes=1 (2026-07-14): runs meaningfully single-node too -- no
# coordination needed at N=1 (barrier is trivially satisfied by 1 party), and
# every mxfs-specific probe in the script is guarded/best-effort (no-ops
# under plain XFS or when absent). Gives a genuine no-coordination-cost floor
# to compare the 2/4/8/16/32-node numbers against. Budget at N=1 is a
# placeholder pending a real ./run.sh 1 xfs / 1 caw calibration measurement.
P6  dir_reuse_coherency   barrier   1   120
# fence_during_write/fault_netpartition measured (2/caw): 17s / 7s.
P6  fence_during_write    fault     2   60
P6  fault_netpartition    fault     2   60

